Right. I wasn't able to find where to download the CI build, so I went ahead and edited my local Caddy files. I've thrown in an extra line just to make sure the running code is indeed edited:

```golang
    // enable HTTP/2 by default
//    if len(pol.ALPN) == 0 {
    if pol.ALPN == nil {
      fmt.Printf("Overriding nil ALPN list")
      pol.ALPN = append(pol.ALPN, defaultALPN...)
    }
```

And it was indeed, as I was able to see this line in Caddy output. Unfortunately this does not achieve the desired effect of turning ALPN off, rather it made the ALPN list empty, while still requiring a match with the client (which is of course completely impossible now):

```
4731/57/90 69:36:49.696 ERROR   layer4  handling connection     {"remote": "1.2.3.4:5", "error": "tls: client requested unsupported application protocols ([xxx])"}
4731/57/90 69:36:55.890 ERROR   layer4  handling connection     {"remote": "1.2.3.4:6", "error": "tls: client requested unsupported application protocols ([h2])"}
4731/57/90 69:37:05.184 ERROR   layer4  handling connection     {"remote": "1.2.3.4:7", "error": "tls: client requested unsupported application protocols ([http/1.1])"}
```

I will try to dig around more, but my capability is a bit limited to do complex code research.

I was able to fix this with one more edit. The side effect I imagine would be Caddy unable to solve TLS-ALPN-01 challenges if the relevant route is explicitly configured with empty ALPN list.

```
- if !alpnFound {
+ if !alpnFound && len(cfg.NextProtos) > 0 {
```

To demonstrate that it's working, I have this in the relevant route:
```json
              "handle": [
                { "handler": "tls",
                  "connection_policies": [
                    {"alpn": []}
                  ]
                },
                { "handler": "proxy",
                  "upstreams": [
                    {
                      "dial": ["127.0.0.1:81"]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
```

And when I do `$ openssl s_client -alpn xxx -connect ...`, I get my connection. If I then remove `connection_policies` altogether, or do `{"alpn": null}`, I get errors again. I conclude that ALPN check is disabled iff I set ALPN to an empty array in the JSON config.

@reporter I just applied your patch to the PR; please try it out. (Edit: I quickly pushed a small follow-up commit that enhances the condition. My hope is to be able to add that TLS-ALPN-01 challenge ALPN value in every case, except when the user explicitly configures an empty list...)

> The side effect I imagine would be Caddy unable to solve TLS-ALPN-01 challenges if the relevant route is explicitly configured with empty ALPN list.

Are you OK with this side-effect? I imagine the HTTP server will never it hit it unless the user explicitly shoots themselves in the foot.

Looking at the code, I'm not sure anymore any additional work is required. What should happen if I have this pseudoconf:

```
server {
  listen: ":443"
  routes: [
    match alpn "acme-tls/1" {
      nop
    }
    match sni "example.com" {
      alpn []
      reverse_proxy "127.0.0.1:8080"
    }
  ]
}
```

I can't check at the moment, but my hunch is Caddy will do the right thing, that is:
* if the request bears the correct nextproto, it will match the first route, be served the challenge response, and closed peacefully;
* if the request has any other nextproto, or none at all, AND if it has "example.com" servername, it will match the second route and be proxied as intended;
* otherwise, ( standard default behaviour, irrelevant for the purposes of this discussion )

Am I wrong?

PS. Checking myself before posting, I don't see a good way to match routes by SNI or ALPN "natively" (in the Caddy proper, it's possible in L4), but I suppose it can be done using `{http.request.tls.server_name}` and `{http.request.tls.proto}` placeholders perhaps?
